course: dictionary
lesson: 9503
title: Stereotypes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A stereotype is a fixed, too-simple idea about what all people in one group are like. You can use it as a noun (a stereotype) or as a verb (to stereotype people). «Spoilt only child» is a stereotype, because many only children do plenty of chores.
explain: To stereotype people means to judge them by that simple idea instead of as the person they really are. Ask yourself: does this idea fit every person in the group, or only a picture in someone's head? A stereotype is usually not a fact.
explain: In stories, a stereotype can also be a character who is flat, not real or deep, and is made from one simple idea. Stereotypes are often linked with unfair treatment, so spotting them in adverts, books and jokes helps you judge people fairly.
fact: A stereotype is a fixed, too-simple idea about all people of one group.
fact: «Lots of older people use technology well»; a rule that all old people are slow is a stereotype.
fact: To stereotype people means to judge them by a group idea instead of as individuals.
fact: A stereotype is usually not a fact, because it does not fit every member of the group.
fact: Useful phrases: «challenge stereotypes», «break the stereotype», «a gender stereotype».
exercise: What is a stereotype? | A picture book for young children | A long word for a story | A true fact about one named person | *A fixed, too-simple idea about what all people of one group are like
exercise: What does it mean to stereotype people? | *To judge them by a simple group idea instead of as the person they really are | To give them a present on their birthday | To get to know them slowly over many years
exercise: Which of these is a stereotype? | *All old people are slow. | Dogs have four legs. | Ms Patel teaches maths at our school. | My friend likes drawing dinosaurs.
test: «A stereotype is a fixed idea about a whole group of people; it is usually not a fact.» What is usually true of a stereotype? | It is the name of one person | It is always a fact | *It is usually not a fact | It is a kind of sport
test: Which character is a flat character made from a simple idea? | A hero who makes a big mistake | A villain who has a hidden, sad past | A talking tree that helps a lost child | *A villain who is cruel just because he is the villain
exam: Which phrase means proving a simple idea wrong? | believe the stereotype | fit the stereotype | copy the stereotype | *break the stereotype
